      "side": 1,
      "message": "In `UsbComm::Wait()`, the code relies on a retry counter (`retries`) to bound the wait time for a specific interrupt. However, if the device returns an unexpected interrupt payload, the code simply falls through the `if/else` block without incrementing `retries`. If the device spams invalid interrupts, the loop will spin infinitely, causing the flash tool to hang permanently. Incrementing the counter on unexpected payloads ensures the loop eventually terminates and returns an error.\n\n### Detailed Fix\n\n**Location:** `util/egis/usb_comm.cc` inside `UsbComm::Wait()`\n\nUpdate the `rc \u003d\u003d 0` block to increment `retries` when the interrupt is successfully received but doesn\u0027t match the expected `kResponseReadyIrq`.\n\n```c\n  while (retries \u003c kMaxRetries) {\n    int rc \u003d libusb_interrupt_transfer(handle_.get(), kEpInInt, irq_buf.data(),\n                                       irq_buf.size(), \u0026transferred,\n                                       kTransferTimeout.count());\n\n    if (rc \u003d\u003d 0) {\n      if (transferred \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 irq_buf[0] \u003d\u003d kResponseReadyIrq) {\n        return {};\n      }\n      // Fix: Unexpected IRQ payload received; count as a retry\n      retries++;\n    } else if (rc \u003d\u003d LIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT) {\n      retries++;\n    } else {\n      std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Interrupt IN error: {}\",\n                   LibusbErrorToString(rc));\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n```",
